commit bodies: 2727495 coordination: serialize approved merges and deployments

Keep development work independent while admitting short resource claims
through a deterministic durable queue. Retain machine reservations across
deployed review and iteration, and preserve uncertain attempts for exact
reconciliation instead of replaying mutations.

Retain reviewed sources, native notification identity and package contracts
so independent systemd workers survive workspace self-deployment. New private
schema-1 state fences pre-contract package selection; ordinary manifests and
existing submission ledgers remain unchanged.

0264c6b portal: explain operations and session machine reservations

Show reserved machines, active work and recent results separately so users
can distinguish completed deployment steps from an ongoing review hold.
Place Operations above Automatic archival in the workspace menu.

Name held machines in session badges, list their states in tooltips and
provide a Reservations tab with incarnation-specific history. Keep the
existing authenticated status surface and safe text rendering. Preserve
expanded details and the last successful view across status refreshes.

ef4ec58 coordination: record optional display dates after ledger publication

Keep creation, state-change, completion and reservation-grant dates in a
bounded private sidecar associated with each entity revision and state.
Publish the ledger first so an interrupted transaction cannot attribute
an abandoned event time to a later retry.

Timing failures leave authority and scheduler wake-up intact. Predecessor
packages ignore the sidecar; missing or stale dates remain unavailable.
Expose these dates only in the read-only portal projection.

a10af6c sessions: use the command runner for native provider arguments

Provider-enabled session creation and terminal recovery called an
uninitialized runner when requesting workspace-host native arguments. Use
the retained command runner and unpack its stdout before validating the
JSON response, so terminal startup can resume the existing shared thread.

Cover initial creation with configured provider arguments and reject invalid
arguments before terminal dispatch. State formats and recovery ownership
remain unchanged.


